---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
import Scaffold from '../components/Scaffold.astro';

// Three authentic-style draft→revision pairs. The visitor taps the part of
// the revision where the interesting change happened. Each explanation
// names the move — that naming is the pedagogy.
const items = [
  {
    move: 'Hedging',
    draft: 'Social media use causes anxiety in teenagers.',
    segments: [
      { text: 'Social media use' },
      { text: 'may contribute to', hit: true },
      { text: 'anxiety in' },
      { text: 'some', hit: true },
      { text: 'adolescents.' },
    ],
    explain:
      'The revision hedges. “Causes” claims certainty the evidence rarely supports; “may contribute to” and “some” shrink the claim to what can be defended. Academic writers are not being timid here — they are being precise about how much they know.',
    miss: 'That part barely changed. Look for where the claim got more careful.',
  },
  {
    move: 'Nominalisation',
    draft: 'Because the government decided to raise interest rates, house prices fell quickly.',
    segments: [
      { text: 'The government’s decision', hit: true },
      { text: 'to raise interest rates led to' },
      { text: 'a rapid fall', hit: true },
      { text: 'in house prices.' },
    ],
    explain:
      'Actions became things: “decided” → “decision”, “fell quickly” → “a rapid fall”. Nominalisation lets one sentence carry what took a whole clause before — it is how academic prose gets dense, for better and sometimes worse.',
    miss: 'Not quite — find where a verb turned into a noun.',
  },
  {
    move: 'Stance',
    draft: 'This proves that the vaccine is effective.',
    segments: [
      { text: 'These findings' },
      { text: 'suggest', hit: true },
      { text: 'that the vaccine is effective' },
      { text: '(Chen et al., 2024).', hit: true },
    ],
    explain:
      '“Proves” became “suggest”, and a citation arrived. The writer has stopped speaking for the universe and started reporting a relationship between specific evidence and a claim — which is what an academic voice is.',
    miss: 'Look at how sure the writer sounds, and on whose authority.',
  },
];
---
<Base title="Notice — Paul Hendrie" description="In-sessional EAP in one minute: spot what changed between draft and revision.">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="kicker">Notice</p>
    <h1 class="ignite">Spot the move</h1>
    <p>Below are three sentences, each shown as a first draft and as the
    revision an academic writer actually submitted. In each revision,
    <strong>tap the part where the interesting change happened</strong>.</p>

    <Scaffold>
      Hint for the first one: read both versions aloud. The change you can
      <em>hear</em> — the one that changes how sure the writer sounds — is
      the one to tap.
    </Scaffold>

    <ol class="activity">
      {items.map((item, i) => (
        <li class="item" data-item={i}>
          <p class="draft"><span class="label">Draft</span> {item.draft}</p>
          <p class="revision">
            <span class="label">Revision</span>
            {item.segments.map((seg) => (
              <button type="button" class="seg" data-hit={seg.hit ? 'true' : 'false'}>{seg.text}</button>
            ))}
          </p>
          <p class="feedback note" data-explain={item.explain} data-miss={item.miss} data-move={item.move} aria-live="polite"></p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <div class="finale" hidden>
      <hr />
      <p>That's in-sessional EAP in one minute: authentic text, a reason to
      look closely, and a name for what you saw. You just did the noticing
      yourself — nobody told you the rule first, and that order matters.</p>
      <p>Designing that experience at course scale — with AI in the loop
      generating the texts, and the pedagogy deciding everything else — is
      <a href="/work/">what I do</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <Note>
      No score, no streak, no confetti. You noticed three things; that was
      the whole design. If you want the theory, the moves are hedging,
      nominalisation and stance — load-bearing walls of academic style.
    </Note>
  </div>

  <style>
    .activity { list-style: none; margin: 2.5rem 0 0; padding: 0; }
    .item { border-top: 1px solid var(--panel-edge); padding: 1.6rem 0 0.6rem; }
    .label {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 0.6rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.16em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--orange);
      display: inline-block;
      width: 5rem;
    }
    .draft { color: var(--dim); }
    .revision { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.3rem 0.35rem; align-items: baseline; }
    .seg {
      font: inherit;
      color: var(--text);
      background: none;
      border: none;
      padding: 0 0.1em;
      margin: 0;
      cursor: pointer;
      border-radius: 2px;
      border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--panel-edge);
      transition: color 0.2s ease, text-shadow 0.2s ease;
    }
    .seg:hover, .seg:focus-visible { color: var(--cyan-core); border-bottom-color: var(--cyan); }
    .seg:focus-visible { outline: 1px solid var(--cyan); outline-offset: 2px; }
    .item.solved .seg { cursor: default; border-bottom-color: transparent; }
    .item.solved .seg[data-hit='true'] {
      color: var(--cyan-core);
      text-shadow: 0 0 12px var(--cyan-glow);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--cyan);
    }
    .feedback { min-height: 1.2rem; }
    .feedback:empty { display: none; }
    @media (min-width: 68rem) {
      /* feedback behaves like a margin note, arriving beside its sentence */
      .feedback { margin-top: -3rem; }
    }
    /* :global — this span is injected by the script, so scoped styles miss it */
    .feedback :global(.move-name) {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-weight: 500;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em;
      font-size: 0.6rem;
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
      color: var(--orange);
      text-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--orange-glow);
    }
    .finale p { max-width: var(--measure); }
  </style>

  <script>
    const items = document.querySelectorAll('.item');
    const finale = document.querySelector('.finale');
    let solved = 0;

    items.forEach((item) => {
      const feedback = item.querySelector('.feedback');
      item.querySelectorAll('.seg').forEach((seg) => {
        seg.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (item.classList.contains('solved')) return;
          if (seg.dataset.hit === 'true') {
            item.classList.add('solved');
            feedback.innerHTML =
              `<span class="move-name">The move: ${feedback.dataset.move}</span>` +
              feedback.dataset.explain;
            if (++solved === items.length && finale) {
              finale.hidden = false;
              finale.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'nearest' });
            }
          } else {
            feedback.textContent = feedback.dataset.miss;
          }
        });
      });
    });
  </script>
</Base>
